    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside outfield configuration, led by **James Wood** (74.15) and **Seiya Suzuki** (73.55). **James Wood** offers the premier play of the day, capitalizing on his elite .404 xwOBA and a favorable matchup against Houser, whose 4.78 xERA provides the perfect runway for a high-production afternoon. Similarly, **Seiya Suzuki** benefits from a 14% park adjustment and a soft matchup against Sugano. The most compelling value play, however, is **Max Muncy** (66.89), whose score is significantly bolstered by a +19% SP skill adjustment against the vulnerable Gasser. While we are navigating the scarcity of SB, the trio of **Corbin Carroll**, **Brice Turang**, and **Travis Bazzana** provides enough speed-based floor to keep our category metrics stable even as we lean into the power upside of **Josh Naylor** (66.88) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (66.21).\n\nThe most difficult decision on the morning involved **Kyle Karros**, who landed on the bench with a 52.47 rating. While **Kyle Karros** benefits from a +14% park factor, he was ultimately edged out by the superior SP-skill matchups afforded to our starting utility players. We are keeping a close eye on the volatile nature of the **Cole Young** and **Josh Naylor** matchup against Rogers, as both are carrying a -7% BvP penalty that suppresses their raw output. Furthermore, **Dalton Rushing** was quickly relegated to the bench (37.50) to preserve game caps, as his encounter with Skenes\u2014an elite arm with a 2.95 xERA\u2014presents too much risk for a sub-40 efficiency score. Our primary tactical concern remains the volatility of the bottom half of the order; we need **Cole Young** to overcome his -6% order penalty to ensure we don't bleed production in the late innings.",
